                <pb facs="0393" n="287" sameAs="frus:frus1891/pg287"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Egan</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1891-01-16"><hi rendition="#italic">January 16,
                        1892</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Blaine points to essential differences between anything maintained by Mr.
                    Webster and what was done by Mr. Matta when, in his message of the 11th
                    December, he instructed Mr. Montt to have that document printed in the United
                    States; and, referring to the palpable insults found in it against the President
                    and other officers of the United States, says that, in his opinion, the
                    transmission of the circular was unprecedented. He declares that the desired
                    withdrawal by President Montt of everything of a discourteous character should
                    be done freely and in suitable terms by Chile. He enjoins prompt action.</p>
            </div>
